WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.209 --> 00:00:03.795
♪♪

00:00:03.795 --> 00:00:08.091
Mike Gunton:
I remember I was standing on a
mountain with David Attenborough for the Africa series.

00:00:08.091 --> 00:00:10.677
♪♪

00:00:10.677 --> 00:00:15.057
Mike Gunton:
One of the lines he was saying
was, “Nowhere on the planet does wildlife put on a greater show.”

00:00:15.057 --> 00:00:16.975
♪♪

00:00:16.975 --> 00:00:18.477
Mike Gunton:
And I started thinking about, 

00:00:18.477 --> 00:00:21.855
Mike Gunton:
“I wonder what it would have been like
to have stood here when the 

00:00:21.855 --> 00:00:24.650
Mike Gunton:
dinosaurs and those creatures
were at their finest moment.”

00:00:24.650 --> 00:00:26.902
♪♪

00:00:26.902 --> 00:00:30.530
And then I thought, “I wonder
if we could actually make a show
like that about dinosaurs.”

00:00:30.530 --> 00:00:32.699
♪♪

00:00:32.699 --> 00:00:34.576
(Roaring)

00:00:34.576 --> 00:00:39.790
♪♪

00:00:39.790 --> 00:00:42.918
Mike Gunton:
It’s taken me 10 years to get
the right people together,

00:00:42.918 --> 00:00:46.296
Mike Gunton:
to find the right stories,
to find the right environments to make it.

00:00:46.296 --> 00:00:49.466
♪♪

00:00:49.466 --> 00:00:52.094
Jon Favreau:
When the collaborating with Mike
Gunton came up,

00:00:52.094 --> 00:00:54.012
Jon Favreau:
it would seemed like a fascinating
prospect.

00:00:54.012 --> 00:00:54.846
♪♪

00:00:54.846 --> 00:00:58.850
How could we show dinosaurs as
though we were filming them in
nature today?

00:00:58.850 --> 00:01:02.813
♪♪

00:01:02.813 --> 00:01:06.358
Mike Gunton:
We decided right up front
this would be real environments.

00:01:06.358 --> 00:01:07.693
♪♪

00:01:07.693 --> 00:01:10.612
Mike Gunton:
That’s influenced both the
stories we’ve told,

00:01:10.612 --> 00:01:12.698
Mike Gunton:
the way we’ve told those
stories, the way we shot it

00:01:12.698 --> 00:01:14.616
Mike Gunton:
the whole ethos of the series.

00:01:14.616 --> 00:01:15.951
♪♪

00:01:15.951 --> 00:01:19.246
Mike Gunton:
When you watch this show, what
you’re seeing in a way is just the tip of an iceberg.

00:01:19.246 --> 00:01:20.205
♪♪

00:01:20.205 --> 00:01:23.333
Mike Gunton:
Because we have got so many
people helping us on this,

00:01:23.333 --> 00:01:27.963
experts of all types: 
paleoclimatologists,
palaeobotanists,

00:01:27.963 --> 00:01:29.464
paleo-ethnologists.

00:01:29.464 --> 00:01:31.633
♪♪

00:01:31.633 --> 00:01:34.928
Mike Gunton:
The amount of data that is
around at the moment is fascinating.

00:01:34.928 --> 00:01:38.390
♪♪

00:01:38.390 --> 00:01:41.059
What’s interesting here is how
surprising these behaviors are.

00:01:41.059 --> 00:01:43.478
♪♪

00:01:43.478 --> 00:01:45.147
Jon Favreau:
I think that’s part of the
filmmaking process,

00:01:45.147 --> 00:01:47.065
Jon Favreau:
that you always have to keep the
audience interested

00:01:47.065 --> 00:01:48.859
Jon Favreau:
by presenting them with things
they don’t expect.

00:01:48.859 --> 00:01:51.111
♪♪

00:01:51.111 --> 00:01:55.240
Jon Favreau:
We used the process that they
would’ve used for a documentary,

00:01:55.240 --> 00:01:57.701
Jon Favreau:
which is sending a team out into
the field

00:01:57.701 --> 00:01:59.119
♪♪

00:01:59.119 --> 00:02:04.458
Jon Favreau:
to observe and to photograph an
environment at a certain place at a certain time.

00:02:04.458 --> 00:02:06.585
♪♪

00:02:06.585 --> 00:02:11.757
You just need to look around the
location I’m in now, and see the
elements that we’ve been facing.

00:02:11.757 --> 00:02:14.760
♪♪

00:02:14.760 --> 00:02:17.929
Simon Bell:
We are filming in places that
are inhospitable,

00:02:17.929 --> 00:02:20.724
Simon Bell:
you know, places that are filled
with lava, with fire. 

00:02:20.724 --> 00:02:22.768
♪♪

00:02:22.768 --> 00:02:24.603
Simon Bell:
You’re always looking for a
great shot,

00:02:24.603 --> 00:02:27.230
and almost half-jokingly I, you
know, I invite the pilot,

00:02:27.230 --> 00:02:28.732
you know, “can we-can we land
there? 

00:02:29.191 --> 00:02:30.859
Simon Bell:
A couple hundred meters away
from this volcano,"

00:02:30.859 --> 00:02:33.111
Simon Bell:
you know, half-expecting for
him turn around to say,

00:02:33.111 --> 00:02:34.780
Simon Bell:
“well there’s no way, that’s far
too dangerous.”

00:02:35.697 --> 00:02:38.784
And then my excitement turned to
utter dread,

00:02:40.577 --> 00:02:44.289
uh, when he started to, you know, dip the
nose of the helicopter down and
fly directly towards it.

00:02:44.289 --> 00:02:48.335
♪♪

00:02:48.335 --> 00:02:54.174
People might think, why bother
coming to such, you know,
challenging places to come and film?

00:02:54.174 --> 00:02:56.093
Mike Gunton:
Why not create environments in
the computer?

00:02:57.177 --> 00:03:00.680
Tim Walker: 
The natural world is an
incredible place.

00:03:00.680 --> 00:03:03.850
Trying to recreate that is a
great challenge.

00:03:05.268 --> 00:03:08.480
Tim Walker: 
If the opportunity’s there for
us to actually travel,

00:03:08.480 --> 00:03:13.026
Tim Walker: 
and capture in-camera the real
atmospherics the real lighting conditions, 

00:03:14.194 --> 00:03:16.613
Tim Walker: 
it’s a far more satisfactory
situation

00:03:16.613 --> 00:03:20.617
Tim Walker: 
than trying to recreate the
natural world in a computer.

00:03:20.617 --> 00:03:21.868
♪♪

00:03:21.868 --> 00:03:26.123
Mother nature still delivers
when it comes to... comes to
this.

00:03:26.123 --> 00:03:29.751
Mike Gunton:
It’s so hard to recreate in any
other way other than reality.

00:03:29.751 --> 00:03:34.339
♪♪

00:03:34.339 --> 00:03:36.883
Neil Harvey:
You know, we’re out here with
minimum kit,

00:03:36.883 --> 00:03:40.637
no lights, no–not a great amount
of technology, 

00:03:40.637 --> 00:03:43.932
but sort of slightly, sort of
roughing it in, in the elements.

00:03:43.932 --> 00:03:45.392
♪♪

00:03:45.392 --> 00:03:50.188
Neil Harvey:
But we had to sort of
philosophically adopt a way of shooting this series.

00:03:50.188 --> 00:03:53.817
Neil Harvey:
In a way, we imagined that we
would do if we were around this period.

00:03:53.817 --> 00:03:55.402
♪♪

00:03:55.402 --> 00:04:00.323
Neil Harvey:
Because one of the bespoke
ideas of a natural history wildlife documentary,

00:04:01.199 --> 00:04:06.496
Neil Harvey
is the intimate portrayal of
those animals that we see.

00:04:06.496 --> 00:04:10.000
♪♪

00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:13.128
And that’s where we can bring
our skills as wildlife
filmmakers to bear.

00:04:13.128 --> 00:04:16.089
Sam Bell:
You know, imagining if these
creatures were real, how would we film them?

00:04:16.798 --> 00:04:18.467
Sam Bell:
Would we jump on ski-doos and
follow them?

00:04:18.467 --> 00:04:20.886
Sam Bell:
Would we have the guts to walk
alongside them?

00:04:21.261 --> 00:04:22.929
Sam Bell:
Would we be hiding in the
bushes?

00:04:23.263 --> 00:04:27.309
And that sort of really informed
a lot of the decisions about,
you know, where we would be.

00:04:27.309 --> 00:04:31.688
Quite often we would
deliberately place the camera
a long way away, behind some rocks,

00:04:31.688 --> 00:04:35.317
Neil Harvey:
cause we’re thinking, “well, we
wouldn’t necessarily want to be noticed by these creatures,

00:04:35.734 --> 00:04:37.819
Neil Harvey:
because that might effect their
behavior.”

00:04:38.987 --> 00:04:40.363
Look over this way here.
-Yeah.

00:04:40.363 --> 00:04:41.865
♪♪

00:04:41.865 --> 00:04:47.412
We then do a round of shooting
where we film dinosaur cutouts,

00:04:47.412 --> 00:04:52.959
Tim Walker:
to give the camera operators a
indication of where they need to be focusing.

00:04:52.959 --> 00:04:56.254
♪♪

00:04:56.254 --> 00:04:58.632
Mike Gunton:
The animals are portrayed quite
extraordinarily

00:04:58.632 --> 00:05:00.759
Mike Gunton:
by the way that the artists have
created these creatures.

00:05:00.759 --> 00:05:06.431
You, you look into their eyes
and you can see something’s
going on in these creatures’ heads.

00:05:06.431 --> 00:05:12.604
♪♪

00:05:12.604 --> 00:05:15.398
Jon Favreau:
When the final version of the
shot gets delivered,

00:05:15.398 --> 00:05:18.735
and we finally get to see it
with everything incorporated.

00:05:18.735 --> 00:05:21.196
♪♪

00:05:21.196 --> 00:05:25.575
Jon Favreau:
There’s a magic experience that
you have, when you see it come to life.

00:05:26.326 --> 00:05:28.912
Jon Favreau:
It looks like something you
would see in a nature documentary today.

00:05:28.912 --> 00:05:31.915
♪♪

00:05:31.915 --> 00:05:33.625
Jon Favreau:
And because, at the end of the
day,

00:05:33.625 --> 00:05:36.878
Jon Favreau:
our scientists have the veto
power over anything we do

00:05:36.878 --> 00:05:40.131
Jon Favreau:
if anything doesn’t pass
scientific muster, we rethink it.

00:05:40.715 --> 00:05:43.510
Because we want this to be
scientifically authentic.

00:05:43.510 --> 00:05:45.512
♪♪

00:05:45.512 --> 00:05:48.723
Jon Favreau:
There are certain elements that
just make everything come together.

00:05:49.266 --> 00:05:52.727
Jon Favreau:
In this landscape of so
many visual effects, and so much content,

00:05:53.562 --> 00:05:56.314
Jon Favreau:
what made this one unique and
what we were going for,

00:05:56.773 --> 00:05:58.650
Jon Favreau:
is what you feel when you watch
it.

00:05:58.650 --> 00:06:00.110
♪♪

00:06:00.110 --> 00:06:03.071
And I think when you get that
right, it makes you feel you’re
there.

00:06:03.071 --> 00:06:11.705
♪♪

